ERROR REPORTS

Please send errors and suggestions to Frans Kaashoek and Robert Morris
(kaashoek,rtm@mit.edu).  The main purpose of xv6 is as a teaching
operating system for MIT's 6.1810, so we are more interested in
simplifications and clarifications than new features.

BUILDING AND RUNNING XV6

You will need a RISC-V "newlib" tool chain from
https://github.com/riscv/riscv-gnu-toolchain, and qemu compiled for
riscv64-softmmu.  Once they are installed, and in your shell
search path, you can run "make qemu".

READER-WRITER DEMONSTRATION

Build and run the demonstration from the xv6 shell:

	make qemu
	$ readwrite

The program forks three readers and two writers.  The kernel-backed
reader-writer service is required because fork() copies user memory in xv6;
therefore a normal user-space shared_data would not actually be shared by the
children.  kernel/rwlock.c contains shared_data and read_count, protected by
an xv6 spinlock.  Readers hold the read side while printing and sleeping, so
several reader sections can overlap.  Writers hold the write side while
incrementing, printing, and sleeping, so no reader or other writer can enter.

Readers are admitted while no writer is waiting.  Once a writer is waiting,
at most three readers from the current batch may enter; subsequent readers
wait until the writer completes.  This preserves reader preference when there
is no writer contention while bounding writer delay, so writers cannot starve
indefinitely.

Representative output (the timestamps and PIDs vary):

	reader-writer test: 3 readers, 2 writers
	[t=12 pid=4] READ  start shared_data=0
	[t=12 pid=5] READ  start shared_data=0
	[t=13 pid=6] READ  start shared_data=0
	[t=15 pid=4] READ  end   shared_data=0
	[t=15 pid=5] READ  end   shared_data=0
	[t=15 pid=6] READ  end   shared_data=0
	[t=16 pid=7] WRITE start/end shared_data=1 (exclusive)
	[t=19 pid=8] WRITE start/end shared_data=2 (exclusive)
	[t=22 pid=4] READ  start shared_data=2
	[t=22 pid=5] READ  start shared_data=2
	[t=22 pid=6] READ  start shared_data=2
	reader-writer test complete: final shared_data=8

The three readers have the same value during their overlapping read section,
while each writer is bracketed by a period with no active readers.
